\title{Do Explanations Transfer? Separating Attribution Information from Transformation Effects in Defect Prediction}
\titlerunning{Separating attribution information from transformation effects}
\author{Priyanshu Kumar \and Kumar Rajnish \and Shubham Kumar}
\authorrunning{Priyanshu Kumar et al.}
\institute{Priyanshu Kumar \and Kumar Rajnish \and Shubham Kumar \at Department of Computer Science \& Engineering, Birla Institute of Technology, Mesra, Ranchi, India \\ \email{priyanshu.kumar@bitmesra.ac.in}}
\date{}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\textbf{Context:} Feature attributions are increasingly reused to weight, select or augment software metrics. An improvement over an untreated learner does not isolate what the explanation contributed, because the same operation also changes feature scale, dimensionality or the information channel available to a second-stage model.
\textbf{Objective:} We ask whether source-derived Shapley attributions improve cross-project defect classification beyond controls matched on those properties, and whether an observed augmentation penalty is attributable to the attributions themselves.
\textbf{Method:} Seven documented project families and 54 static code metrics are evaluated leave-one-project-out. Two attribution teachers, four downstream learners, ten source subsamples and two tuning regimes give @@score_records@@ scored predictions across @@arm_count@@ arms. Three of those arms are harness controls that do not appear in prior explanation-transfer evaluations: a featureless reference, a source-label permutation preserving per-project class counts, and a documented adaptation comparator. A fourth is a dimension-matched augmentation control in which the attribution columns are replaced by uninformative columns of the same number and scale. Every reported metric is recomputed from stored predictions.
\textbf{Results:} The harness registers signal removal and addition: the untreated learner exceeds the featureless reference by @@ctl_prevalence@@ ROC-AUC, permuting source labels costs @@ctl_permuted@@, and the adaptation comparator gives @@ctl_coral@@. Against matched controls the attribution operations are small: weighting differs from uniform scaling by @@weight@@ and from permuted weights by @@weight_shuffled@@, and selection differs from equal-sized random selection by @@selection@@. Augmentation is negative, @@augmentation@@ against the prediction-only control; the dimension-matched control attributes @@aug_noise_vs_p@@ of that to added uninformative dimensions alone, leaving @@aug_shap_vs_noise@@ attributable to the attribution columns. Leave-one-target-out aggregation sensitivity is reported for every comparison.
\textbf{Conclusions:} On this benchmark the tested attribution operations contribute little beyond matched controls, while the harness demonstrably responds to signal. The augmentation penalty is @@aug_verdict_short@@. These are descriptive fixed-benchmark results, not equivalence findings, and target rotations share training projects, so they are not independent replications.
\keywords{Software defect prediction \and Cross-project prediction \and Feature attribution \and SHAP \and Controlled evaluation \and Reproducibility}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Software defect prediction ranks or classifies software units to direct quality assurance effort. Cross-project prediction is attractive when a target project has no labelled history, but its effectiveness depends on the relationship between source and target systems, and comparative evaluations must separate the learning algorithm from the data, preprocessing and adaptation choices around it \citep{ref13}.

Feature explanations introduce a further choice. Information extracted from a fitted source model can be reused downstream: a large attribution may decide which metric is kept, how strongly it is scaled, or which additional column is appended. These operations are often discussed together as explanation-guided learning, yet they change different properties of a predictor. Multiplication changes numerical geometry, selection changes dimensionality, and augmentation changes both dimensionality and the information available to a second-stage model.

This creates a problem of experimental identification. If multiplying every input by one constant reproduces the improvement attributed to attribution weighting, the improvement cannot be assigned to the correspondence between feature and attribution. If appending uninformative columns of the same width reproduces the penalty attributed to attribution augmentation, the penalty is a dimensionality effect rather than an attribution effect. An untreated baseline gives context but cannot make either distinction.

A second problem is rarely addressed. When a controlled comparison returns a near-zero difference, that result is only informative if the evaluation could have shown otherwise. Reporting a small effect without demonstrating that the harness responds to signal leaves the reader unable to separate an uninformative treatment from an insensitive measurement. We therefore include arms whose expected behaviour is known in advance: a featureless predictor that should score at chance, a label permutation that should destroy source signal, and a published domain-adaptation method that changes the representation in a documented way \citep{ref3}.

We ask a narrow question: \emph{when does source-derived attribution information improve defect classification beyond a control matched on the property the operation changes?} We compare weighting, selection and augmentation against controls that preserve scale, subset size, or column count and channel. Treatment and control are tuned independently using only source-project validation, and the comparison is repeated under a shared baseline setting.

This study follows an audit of an earlier repository. The audit prompted new execution, and this manuscript reports a second expansion that adds the harness controls, the dimension-matched augmentation control, seven further source subsamples and two further downstream learners. The design was recorded before generating any new model result but after inspecting earlier ones. We describe it as an exploratory extension, not a preregistered confirmation.

Our contributions are an explicit treatment/control design in which every operation has a control matched on the property it changes; harness controls that make a small effect interpretable; a decomposition of the augmentation penalty into a dimensionality component and an attribution component; and a reproducible link from stored predictions to every reported number. We do not propose a new learner, and we do not claim that a small aggregate rules out useful explanations under other conditions.

\section{Background and related work}
\subsection{Attributions and downstream utility}
Shapley additive explanations express a fitted model's output as additive feature contributions under an explanation model \citep{ref1}. Transfer here means reusing source-derived attributions to construct another learner's inputs. It does not mean that one attribution vector is valid for every target model, nor that an attribution is a causal property of software.

Shapley-based feature augmentation studies the use of attribution-derived information in predictive modelling \citep{ref2}. Our prediction-only control isolates the incremental value of attributions beyond a stacking channel, and our dimension-matched control isolates it further from the cost of added columns. A shared source teacher, explicit project exclusion and source-group tuning make this an adaptation rather than an exact reproduction. Explainability-guided augmentation has also been proposed for data-scarce cross-project prediction \citep{ref11}. We do not benchmark every such algorithm; we test whether the chosen attribution correspondence contributes beyond its matched controls.

Interpretation depends on correlated predictors \citep{ref10}, so we retain and report attribution rank stability and source redundancy diagnostics. These describe the fitted representation and do not by themselves explain a predictive effect. Work on developer-behaviour metrics likewise separates explanatory rankings from time-aware predictive evaluation \citep{ref12}; our schema and question differ, since we transform static code metrics rather than introduce behavioural predictors.

\subsection{Controls, adaptation and evaluation design}
Domain adaptation offers a documented alternative use of target information. Correlation alignment whitens source covariance and recolours it with target covariance, using unlabelled target covariates only \citep{ref3}. We include it as a comparator, not as a competitor to be beaten: its role is to show that the harness registers a representation change of known type. We do not assume it must improve accuracy, and we report its outcome on every target.

Public defect benchmarks enable comparison but inherit their collection assumptions \citep{ref4,ref5}. Whether a defect is assigned to a release depends on how affected releases and fixes are identified. The external dataset used here supplies a documented affected-release response and a corresponding loader \citep{ref9}. We use its realistic binary response without treating the name as a guarantee of perfect ground truth.

Model selection is part of the evaluated procedure, and optimising settings on evaluation data introduces selection bias \citep{ref7}. We therefore rebuild representations inside source-group validation and keep the held-out project outside fitting, tuning and attribution construction. Repeated resampling does not by itself supply independent evidence \citep{ref8}; our summaries are conditional on the selected benchmark and distinguish projects from computation repeats throughout.

\section{Research questions and design}
\label{sec:design}
\textbf{RQ1:} Does the evaluation harness register the removal and the addition of predictive signal, as measured by a featureless reference, a source-label permutation and a documented adaptation comparator?

\textbf{RQ2:} On documented external project families, how do source-derived SHAP weighting and selection compare with controls matched on multiplier magnitude, multiplier distribution and subset size, under independent and shared tuning?

\textbf{RQ3:} Is the augmentation penalty attributable to the attribution columns, or to the added dimensions they occupy?

\textbf{RQ4:} How sensitive are the aggregates to attribution teacher, tuning regime, source subsample and the omission of any single target from the aggregate?

Figure~\ref{fig:workflow} shows the information boundary. The target project contributes covariates to prediction and responses only to scoring. The adaptation comparator additionally reads unlabelled target covariates and is the only transductive arm; this is stated wherever it is reported.

@@workflow_figure@@

\section{Data and provenance}
\label{sec:data}
The external data come from the authors' Rnalytica repository, pinned to commit \texttt{1931ca6537b00234b80e3d96f6c76c97524841f2}, with URLs and SHA-256 digests supplied in Online Resource 1. We selected every JIRA family in that inventory absent from the earlier benchmark, excluding Camel and Lucene, and within each family the highest listed release whose name contained neither beta nor preview. This is a deterministic filename rule, not a claim that each selected version was designated production-stable. Table~\ref{tab:data} lists all seven files. No project was removed after observing model performance.

@@dataset_table@@

The source collection describes 54 code metrics alongside process and ownership metrics \citep{ref9}. We use only the explicit 54-column code-metric allowlist, covering complexity, size, coupling and related properties. The response is \texttt{RealBug}, consistent with the authors' loader. Identifiers, heuristic labels, both defect-count columns and all process and ownership columns are excluded, which avoids using defect history to predict defects. The collector's affected-release method supplies a documented label construction; we have not independently re-mined every issue report.

Each file has a unique identifier within its release. Metric-vector repetition and shared path strings across projects are audited separately, since a shared path string does not establish identical source code and identical metrics do not necessarily identify the same file. No response-based duplicate removal is performed.

The original local Eclipse and Jureczko/PROMISE experiments remain supplementary. Those checks establish agreement with curated contents, not the original acquisition history or the observation windows of the bug-history predictors, so the uncertain local results are excluded from the primary evidence and earlier and external scores are never pooled into one estimate.

This is retrospective, release-based cross-project classification. Sources are not filtered by a common calendar cutoff, and retrospective issue labels need not have been available at a historical deployment date, so we make no claim of time-forward operational forecasting.

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Project rotation and preprocessing}
Each of the seven projects serves once as the target, the remaining six as sources. For each of @@seed_count@@ seeds, up to 400 rows are sampled without replacement from each source, stratified by its response, giving 2{,}400 training rows per rotation. The cap gives each source equal weight and bounds computation; it is not a claim that using all rows would give the same result. A predeclared sensitivity comparing 400 against 800 rows is reported separately. All target rows are evaluated, and source indices and all target predictions are saved.

Imputation uses the training median and constant columns are removed using training variation only. Logistic regression receives a signed logarithm followed by robust scaling; tree and boosting learners receive neither. No resampling is applied.

\subsection{Teachers, cross-fitting and arms}
Two attribution teachers, RandomForest and ExtraTrees, are fitted with fixed settings recorded before execution. Within the source training rows a three-fold cross-fitted teacher produces out-of-fold probabilities and out-of-fold attributions, so no row's attribution is derived from a model that saw its label. A teacher refitted on all source rows supplies target probabilities and target attributions. Feature weights are the mean absolute out-of-fold attribution per feature, normalised to unit sum.

Table~\ref{tab:arms} lists all @@arm_count@@ arms. Each treatment has a control matched on the property the treatment changes. Uniform weighting matches the mean multiplier magnitude but carries no attribution information; shuffled weighting matches the multiplier distribution exactly but destroys the feature correspondence; random selection matches subset size; prediction augmentation matches the added-channel structure without attributions; and the dimension-matched augmentation control appends uninformative columns equal in number to the attribution columns, drawn independently from normal distributions whose mean and standard deviation are taken from the corresponding source attribution columns. The featureless reference predicts the source positive rate for every target row and is by construction independent of the downstream learner. The permutation control permutes source labels within each project, preserving per-project class counts, and is tuned on those permuted labels; it is scored against true target labels.

@@arms_table@@

\subsection{Tuning and the information boundary}
Hyperparameters are selected by three-fold GroupKFold over source projects, scored by ROC-AUC, with two candidates per learner and an equal budget for every arm including all controls. Representations and teachers are rebuilt inside every source-validation fold. The independent regime selects per arm; the shared regime reuses the arm-agnostic untreated choice, which isolates a representation change under a common setting from performance under equal tuning budgets.

Target responses enter scoring only. They are never used in fitting, tuning, imputation, feature screening, attribution construction or hyperparameter selection. The function that produces target predictions accepts no target response argument, and this is asserted by test. The adaptation comparator reads unlabelled target covariates, which is disclosed wherever it appears.

\subsection{Estimands, aggregation and what the numbers are not}
For each comparison we form paired differences within identical evaluation cells, average repeats, teachers and learners inside each target, and then take the equal-target mean. Reported ranges across targets describe heterogeneity, not uncertainty.

Leave-one-target-out aggregation sensitivity recomputes that equal-target mean with one project omitted from the aggregate, once per project. It does not retrain anything and does not remove the omitted project from the source pools of the remaining six, so it is a sensitivity of the aggregate and not a project-exclusion experiment. Repeat variability across source subsamples is reported separately.

Target rotations share training projects and repeats share observations, so teacher, learner and seed cells cannot multiply the effective number of projects. We report no significance test over overlapping repeats, no confidence interval, no equivalence bound and no retrospective power estimate. A control that behaves as designed shows that the harness detects signal loss or a representation change of that magnitude; it does not establish power to demonstrate equivalence near zero.

Every reported number is recomputed from stored target predictions, the score grid is checked for completeness and uniqueness, source-project fold separation and out-of-fold coverage are verified, and stored hyperparameter choices are re-derived from stored validation scores. Failures stop the analysis rather than dropping cells.

\section{Results}
\subsection{Does the harness register signal? (RQ1)}
Table~\ref{tab:controls} and Figure~\ref{fig:harness} report the three harness controls. The untreated learner exceeds the featureless reference by @@ctl_prevalence@@ ROC-AUC. Permuting source labels within project, which preserves every class count and removes only the correspondence between features and labels, changes performance by @@ctl_permuted@@. The adaptation comparator changes performance by @@ctl_coral@@.

