\section{Relationship to the earlier experiments}
An earlier execution of this benchmark used three source subsamples, two downstream learners and eight arms, without harness controls or a dimension-matched augmentation control. It is retained in Online Resource 2. Restricting the present execution to the seeds and learners that earlier run also used isolates the effect of the wider grid from the effect of the added arms. @@identity_statement@@ Both restricted and full estimates are reported in the accompanying change report.

@@sourcecap_statement@@ Earlier local bug-history experiments remain supplementary and are never pooled with these results.

\section{Discussion and implications}
\subsection{What counts as evidence of attribution utility}
An improvement over an untreated learner is not evidence that an explanation supplied information, because the operation that applies the explanation also changes scale, dimensionality or channel. The controls used here are cheap: a constant multiplier, a permuted weight vector, an equal-sized random subset, and uninformative columns of matched width. Reporting them alongside the treatment costs one extra run per arm and changes what the comparison can support. We would encourage their use whether or not the resulting effect is small.

\subsection{Why harness controls matter for small effects}
A near-zero difference is uninformative unless the evaluation could have produced something else. The featureless reference and the label permutation establish the lower end of the scale, and the adaptation comparator shows a representation change of documented type moving the same measurement. This is a weaker statement than a power analysis and we do not present it as one: it bounds the kind of effect the design registers, not the smallest effect it could resolve.

\subsection{Scope}
These are descriptive results on one curated collection of seven Java project families with one label construction and one metric schema. They constrain claims about the tested implementations on this benchmark. They do not establish that attribution reuse is useless in general, that other explainers behave the same way, or that a different teacher, budget or feature family would give the same answer.

\section{Threats to validity}
\textbf{Construct validity.} Affected-release labels depend on issue-report accuracy and retrospective linking. The allowlist removes outcome columns and process histories but does not certify every upstream measurement. Static code metrics are proxies for program properties. ROC-AUC, average precision and macro F1 answer different questions and none measures developer utility directly.

\textbf{Internal validity.} The code records transformations, folds, choices and predictions, and validators check their consistency; analysis stops on a failure rather than dropping a cell. Cross-fitting reduces in-sample teacher leakage and source-group tuning excludes the target. Narrow candidate grids, a 400-row source cap, defaults outside those grids and shared random draws nevertheless condition the result. The fourth downstream learner was selected on measured runtime before any result of this execution existed, and that decision is recorded in the protocol.

\textbf{Statistical validity.} Project rotations share training projects and repeats share observations, so teacher, learner and seed cells cannot multiply the effective project count. Ten repetitions bound source-sample variability better than three but do not make targets independent. Rare outcomes, including the small defective count in one project, make individual target scores sensitive to particular files. Target ranges and leave-one-target-out ranges are descriptive; neither is a confidence interval or an equivalence bound. No post hoc power or population significance claim is made.

\textbf{External validity.} External here means new to this repository's earlier experiment, not a probability sample of industrial software. The families come from one curated JIRA collection and some share ecosystems and practices. Different languages, newer systems, other explainers, other feature families and chronological deployment settings remain untested.

\textbf{Researcher degrees of freedom.} This extension was designed after earlier results were known. Its protocol and fingerprints record what was fixed before new execution but are not a public preregistration. All completed arms and both tuning regimes are retained, so no favourable comparison is selectively presented.

